<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Sixth Coalition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Sixth Coalition (1813–1814) was fought, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s disastrous invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, by Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and other states against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Napoleon won several battles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1813 but was decisively beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in October, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into southern France. The allies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in March 1814; Napoleon abdicated and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy was restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-sixth-coalition.jpg" alt="War of the Sixth Coalition" width="320" height="720" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruedi33a · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_sixth_coalition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in June 1812 to compel Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> to remain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> numbered as many as 650,000 men, about half of them French. After the inconclusive Battle of Borodino and the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, which Alexander refused to bargain over, Napoleon was forced to withdraw. The Prussian corps that had been compelled to serve with him was neutralised by the Convention of Tauroggen, signed on 30 December 1812 without the consent of the Prussian king, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kalisz-1813">Treaty of Kalisch</a> on 28 February 1813 ranged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> on the Allied side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> raised new forces and inflicted 40,000 casualties on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> on 2 May and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> on 20 and 21 May 1813, though he lost about as many men. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> ran from 4 June to 13 August, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the Allies. Napoleon won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 26 and 27 August, but at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a>, the Battle of Nations, from 16 to 19 October, a French army reinforced to 191,000 faced more than 430,000 Allied troops. He retreated in fairly good order, though the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a> was blown early and 30,000 men were left to be captured.</p><p>Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>'s army at Vitoria on 21 June 1813. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> won several battles in the Six Days' Campaign in early 1814 with fewer than 80,000 men against a Coalition force of 370,000 to 405,000. At the Treaty of Chaumont on 9 March the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> agreed to keep the Coalition until Napoleon's total defeat. Their armies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 31 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 2 April the French Senate declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> deposed. He wished to fight on, but on 4 April his marshals, led by Ney, refused to march. Napoleon abdicated on 11 April 1814, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> followed on 30 May. He was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, from September 1814 to June 1815, was held to redraw the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Norway declared independence on 17 May 1814, and Bernadotte invaded it in July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Sixth Coalition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
